---
import DocsLayout from '@/layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import { DocsTable, Callout } from '@/molecules/DocComponents/DocComponents';
import MermaidDiagram from '@/components/MermaidDiagram.astro';

const TOKENS = [
  ['Browser session', 'Signed-in user', 'Manager product API', 'Browser cookie'],
  ['Workspace bootstrap', 'One workspace startup', 'Manager registration once', 'Read-only runtime bundle'],
  ['Runtime access and refresh', 'One registered workspace', 'Manager runtime API', 'Workspace process memory'],
  ['Tenant Redis credential', 'One tenant', 'Redis ACL and namespace', 'Mounted bundle / runtime'],
  ['GitHub installation token', 'One authorized repository', 'Git and GitHub API', 'Short-lived workspace process'],
  ['A2A pair token', 'One ticket pair', 'Nexus relay', 'Paired worker parameters'],
];
---

<DocsLayout breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Docs', href: '/docs' }, { label: 'Architecture', href: '/docs/architecture' }, { label: 'Credentials and Trust' }]}>
  <h1>Credentials and trust boundaries</h1>
  <p>These credentials are separate. A browser session cannot register a workspace; a runtime token cannot access Redis; an A2A token cannot call Manager APIs.</p>
  <DocsTable headers={['Credential', 'Bound to', 'Accepted by', 'Where the raw value appears']} rows={TOKENS} />

  <h2 id="registration">Registration and rotation</h2>
  <p>Manager hashes bootstrap, access, and refresh credentials in PostgreSQL. Registration atomically consumes the one-time bootstrap. The runtime uses its access token on Manager calls. When it receives an authorization failure, it rotates the refresh token and retries once. The refresh family has a fixed maximum lifetime.</p>

  <h2 id="broker">GitHub credential broker</h2>
  <MermaidDiagram title="Brokered GitHub token exchange" source={`sequenceDiagram
    participant W as Tenant workspace
    participant M as Manager broker
    participant P as PostgreSQL
    participant G as GitHub App
    W->>M: Runtime token + purpose git or api
    M->>P: Validate workspace, tenant and repository
    M->>G: Sign App request and mint installation token
    G-->>M: Short-lived scoped token
    M->>P: Record encrypted credential lease
    M-->>W: Short-lived token for authorized repository`} />
  <p>The runtime calls <code>POST /api/v1/runtime/github-credentials</code> with purpose <code>git</code> or <code>api</code>. Manager derives the repository, installation, and permission profile from the authenticated workspace. It signs a GitHub App request using the server-held private key, validates the returned scope, records a lease, and returns a short-lived installation token.</p>
  <p>Hosted GitHub operations use the App installation identity. A person signs in with GitHub to access OpenFlows and connect the organization; that OAuth login is not the credential Forge uses to push a branch.</p>

  <h2 id="workspace-exposure">What a compromised workspace can see</h2>
  <p>A running workspace can read its own bootstrap bundle, tenant Redis credential, current runtime session, and any short-lived GitHub token it has requested. Treat it as a credential-bearing execution environment. It does not receive the Manager master key, GitHub App private key, global Coder operator credential, or another tenant's Redis password.</p>
  <Callout type="warning" title="Operational boundary">Short-lived tokens reduce exposure; they do not make a compromised workspace harmless. Network access, workspace isolation, revocation, audit, and credential rotation remain necessary.</Callout>
</DocsLayout>
